ime period that lasted for  multiple quarters each for example let's  take a look at the recession that took  place between 1974 and 1975. during this  time period the s p 500 got obliterated  dropping a full 50  but what do you think happened to  housing given this massive sell-off in  the stock market you would at least  expect a cooldown in real estate right  well the median sale price actually  increased from 34 000 to 39 000  in other words housing grew by 14.7  percent while the country experienced a  recession and the s p 500 dropped by 50  and this isn't a one-off thing either  this happened on multiple occasions  during this time period for instance we  saw another recession during the first  half of 1980. this recession was  accompanied by 20 sell-off in the stock  market but housing prices still went up  similarly in the 1981-1982 recession the  stock market sold off 25  this time the housing market actually  had a slight pullback coming in at 5.7  percent but nonetheless housing prices  were still higher at the end of the  recession than at the beginning of the  recession as you can see just because  there's a recession in the stock market  crashes does not mean that the housing  market must crash as well something else  to note is that a lot of people think  that the rate of increase in housing  prices are indicative of a housing crash  but this is not really true either  throughout the entire decade of the  1970s we saw abnormally high growth in  real estate prices when president nixon  dropped the gold standard in 1971 the  median sale price stood at twenty five  thousand eight hundred dollars ten years  later the median sale price sixty nine  thousand four hundred dollars this means  that housing prices grew 168  during these 10 years that translates to  an annual return of 10.4 for an entire  decade while that might be hard to  believe at first glance it makes perfect  sense when you account for inflation the  average inflation rate during these 10  years was a whopping 8.42  so really real estate only grew about  two percent per year during this time  period which is very sustainable the  thing to keep in mind is that real  estate is one of the most trusted and  reliable safe havens to protect against  inflation so it only makes sense that  real estate would at the very least keep  pace with inflation regardless of  whether there's a recession or stock  market crash so don't think that housing  prices have to come down just because  they've gone up so fast as long as  inflation is high that scenario remains  unlikely  another popular argument for a housing  market crash is rising interest rates  people argue that as interest rates go  up people will be able to afford less  meaning that sellers will have to  decrease prices while this argument  makes sense during normal periods the  thing to consider is that supply is  extraordinarily low  housing inventory is literally at  40-year lows and the trend has only been  downwards so it's not clear how much  rising interest rates will even affect  housing prices for example let's say  that 30-year mortgage rates go all the  way to eight percent from their lows of  about three percent the general rule is  that for every one percent increase in  mortgage rates purchasing power  decreases by roughly 10 percent so a  five percent increase in mortgage rates  would decrease purchasing power by 50  now that's quite a decrease but even if  this results in inventory doubling will  only be back to pre-pandemic levels in  other words the decrease in purchasing  power would more or less just wipe out  the excess demand so while houses may no  longer be selling for crazy amounts  above listing i'm not sure this would  drag down the listing prices themselves  all that much something else to note is  that even with these massive hikes  interest rates are still super low on a  historical basis and even more  importantly real mortgage rates are  still negative while a six percent  mortgage rate may sound high compared to  12 months ago six percent is still lower  than the eight percent inflation that  we've been seeing so from an investment  perspective taking out a mortgage right  now is still rather appealing and the  last thing to consider regarding  mortgage rates is that they're  completely irrelevant to a significant  amount of buyers because they're buying  with cash now this isn't to say that  they don't keep tabs on mortgage rates  but in terms of purchasing power it does  not affect them whatsoever i'm sure  these guys would like for the real  estate market to cool off as well so  that they can buy lower but most of them  are not going to wait for a crash  because they have excess cash reserves  that need protection from inflation i  mean these 13 companies alone hold a  trillion dollars in cash  now apple is likely not going to go out  and pick up single family homes but  banks and financial institutions will  and that's exactly what they've been  doing in q4 of 2021 for example almost  20 of all u.s home sales were to  institutional investors who are more  than likely paying cash so rising  mortgage rates probably won't cause a  housing market collapse and if you don't  believe me just take a look at the 1980s  after a decade of high inflation in the  1970s the fed decided to jack up  interest rates to unprecedented amounts  this caused 30-year mortgage rates to  nearly reach 20 percent  given that mortgage rates were at 8.5  just a few years before this this means  that mortgage rates rose 10 percent  according to a rule of thumb purchasing  power should have decreased by 100 and  housing prices should have gotten flamed  but in reality real estate prices  actually ended up rallying 100 percent  over the next decade so if an 18  mortgage rate did not cause a crash  there's no guarantee that a 78 mortgage  rate will  yet another factor thus preventing a  housing crash from taking place is that  home buyers are more qualified than ever  before here's the thing for 2008 cell  collapse to take place there needs to be  a fundamental crisis secular variations  in supply and demand due to mortgage  rates and inflation are not going to  crash the housing market 40 within 12  months for that you need a fundamental  crisis like loads of unqualified buyers  and low cash reserves across financial  institutions but we're not seeing that  we're actually seeing the exact opposite  the average home buyer is more qualified  than ever and the reason for this is  that the average person can no longer  afford these homes so it's primarily  just high-income families that are  buying homes in texas for example the  median home buyer has a household income  of a hundred and twelve thousand dollars  this puts the average home buyer in  texas in the 71 percent talent in terms  of household income in other words the  majority of homes are being sold to  people who are well off so it's quite  unlikely that we're going to see a  crisis where people suddenly can't pay  their mortgages now you might be saying  that these people may lose their jobs  during a recession which could change  their financial position but the truth  is the vast majority of job losses are  usually amongst everyday blue collar  workers white-collar workers are  generally some of the last people to  lose their jobs because of two reasons  one these employees are harder to  replace so companies are more reluctant  to let them go  and two most of these guys work at cash  with id companies that can afford to  weather a recession  also even if the company cannot survive  the recession it's more than likely that  the home buyer can survive it themselves  the median bank account balance rises  exponentially with income for example  the median banking balance in the us is  only 5 300  however the median bank on balance of  someone earning between 90 and 100 000  is 70 000  the median home buyer in texas actually  makes even more than this so it's likely  that they have nearly a hundred thousand  dollars in savings the median monthly  payment on a new mortgage was 1931 in  february  even if we say that's 2500 these median  home buyers could keep up their mortgage  payments for two full years before  running into problems also it's not just  home buyers who are on top of their  finances banks are on top of their  finances as well  jp morgan chase for example is holding  1.5 trillion dollars in cash and bank of  america has 895 billion in cash these  institutions and home buyers are more  liquidated than ever before meaning that  the odds of a fundamental crisis are  also lower than ever before  in the end there's no question that  housing is becoming increasingly  unaffordable for the average person but  this does not mean that the housing  market is going to collapse anytime soon  it appears that many people are hoping  for a massive plummet so that they can  finally buy for a reasonable price but  the truth is that 2008 was a one-off  thing i mean just take a look at the  last 60 years worth of pricing data  there are times where the market goes  sideways or cools off 10-15 percent but  2008 was the only time that the housing  market actually crashed since then  regulation has tightened significantly  and lenders have become extremely  cautious so it's primarily just  high-income families and cash buyers who  are driving the current market on top of  this from the 1970s and 80s we've seen  that real estate can continue to  skyrocket despite recessions stock  market crashes and interest rate hikes  now this isn't to say that we won't see  a cool off like we've already been  seeing  as mortgage rates rise and inventory  builds up the real estate market will no  doubt soften but to correlate that to a  full-on housing crash is quite a leap in  logic to be honest the vast majority of  articles and videos are telling people  that there's going to be a monumental  housing crash or just preying on these  people's desperateness to afford a house  but that's just what i think and i'm  just some random guy on the internet do  you guys think that there's going to be  a housing collapse comment that down  below  also drop a like if you hope that  inflation doesn't get much worse and of  course consider checking out our discord  community to suggest for your video  ideas and consider subscribing to see  more questions logically answered but  until then i'm hurry and i'll see you  guys on the next one  you

